WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:17.820
Up until the 19th century, England only had
two universities, that of Cambridge and Oxford.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:23.150
This meant that for much of English history
the educated elites, who were at the center

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:28.489
of political, social, and economic affairs
of the country, were often educated at either

00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:34.691
Cambridge or Oxford. Even when new universities
started to be established, like the University

00:00:34.691 --> 00:00:40.250
of Durham or the University of London, Oxford
and Cambridge still retained their ‘exclusive’

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.620
‘elite’ status. As historian Caroline
Elkins put it, ‘Oxford and Cambridge were

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:51.670
natural codas for British ruling-class education’.
These two universities even had, up until

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:58.430
1950, their own two MPs (each) in the British
Parliament; and so, with their status as the

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.230
educational institutions for the ‘elites’,
and their direct connection to Parliament,

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.810
it should not be surprising that Oxford and
Cambridge were directly connected to, and

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:13.909
benefited from, the establishment and running
of the British Empire.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:17.080
Some Scottish Universities, however, and exclusive
Public Schools (and later English Universities

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.300
like London and Durham), also had their own
connections to the British Empire, it wasn't

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.970
just the universities of Oxford and Cambridge.
With that said, the University of Oxford’s

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:30.310
imperial connections are often touted as the
most expansive. Being the oldest University

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:36.380
in the English speaking world, and being more
focused on humanities than Cambridge was,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:42.410
Oxford usually attracted the most politically
active students. Therefore, as Brockliss states

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:48.340
… ‘Much can and should be made of Oxford
alumni’s domination of the top posts in

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:54.140
the government and the civil service, and
the role this played in securing the University's

00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:00.510
position.’ A position, which made ‘Oxford's
connection with the [British] Empire as old

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:05.370
as the Empire itself’.
But, how did this University of Oxford’s

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:12.019
connection with the Empire actually look like,
and what are the Imperial legacies of the

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:19.030
University today? Well, let's find out …

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.640
The history of the British Empire usually
begins with the 16th century Tudor conquest

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.670
of Ireland which also coincided with the English
expeditions into the Americas. To what extent

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.600
the University of Oxford was actually needed
in these early days of the Empire is hard

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.530
to say. Many ruling class elites would have
participated in English imperial enterprises

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.510
whether they went to Oxford or not. But, it
was at the University of Oxford where many

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:51.370
ruling class elites acquired an academic education
which often proved useful for, among other

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:56.770
things, conducting said imperial enterprises.
The English crown quickly recognized this

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:02.510
usefulness of having academically educated
men in the state service and so, in the 16th

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.510
century, it increasingly turned to the University
when it wanted a supply of academically educated

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:13.790
men. For example, Elizabeth the first, during
her visit to Oxford in 1566, requested that

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.020
the heads of the University help her recruit
more Oxford educated men into her service.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:24.459
This action resulted in Oxford educated men
like Thomas Bodley, Walter Raleigh, and Humphrey

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:30.250
Gilbert playing vital roles in Elizabeth's
government. Bodley was an important diplomat

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:36.170
while Raleigh and Gilbert partook in the Elizabethan
conquest of Ireland and both subsequently

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:41.760
gained plantations in Ireland from that conquest.
Raleigh and Gilbert also conducted several

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.150
expeditions into the Americas. All of these
Imperial enterprises, on part of Raleigh and

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:52.760
Gilbert, required a degree of violence, after
all, as historian Delahaye states, colonization

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.120
always ‘carried with it a degree of violence’.
But, it was in Ireland that this violence

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.650
turned particularly brutal. Humphrey Gilbert,
who was called ‘England’s first Empire

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:08.959
Builder’ and ‘Elizabeth’s Racketeer’,
partook in a systematic war of terror in southern

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:14.770
Ireland killing all the Irish that dared stand
against him. In his own words, if the Irish

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:20.690
do not surrender ‘[I will] win it by force,
how many lives so ever it cost, putting man,

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:25.550
woman, and child of them to the sword … Being
for my part constantly of this opinion that

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:31.870
no conquered nation will ever yield willingly
their obedience for love but rather for fear’.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.370
Another notable University of Oxford graduate
from this time was Thomas Harriot. He worked

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:41.490
closely with Sir Walter Raleigh and, when
Raleigh captured and brought back to England

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:46.680
a couple of Carolina Algonquin Indians, Harriot
thoroughly questioned them on the New World

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.830
and learned their language; becoming the first
man in England to learn a native American

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:57.449
Language. Harriot also had extensive navigational,
mathematical, and cartographic skills; all

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:02.889
of which made him an instrumental figure in
the establishment of the eventually unsuccessful

00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:08.530
Roanoke Colony. Harriot's navigational guides
and maps were used by yet another notable

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:14.710
Oxford alumnus, Thomas Roe, who, using Harriot's
maps, went into the Amazon in search of the

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:20.680
mythical El dorado. With that said, Thomas
Roe is actually more well remembered for being

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.540
the first official royal English ambassador
sent to the Mughal Empire in 1615. This was

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:31.680
done at the behest of the British East India
Company. But, more on the East India Company

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:32.680
later.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.180
As the English state continued to pursue ever
more imperial enterprises all around the world,

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:43.010
it needed an ever increasing number of educated
men who could act as diplomats like Roe, as

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:48.590
explorers like Raleigh, as cartographers and
polyglots like Harriot, and as imperialists

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:54.510
like Gilbert. Therefore, as long as the crown
continued to pursue imperial enterprises,

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:59.470
it also continued to acknowledge and support
the University of Oxford’s role as a necessary

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.450
provider of educated men for those enterprises.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:08.460
This support by the crown can be seen, for
example, when James the fourth of Scotland

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.740
and first of England came to power. He was,
at least on paper, the first ruler of the

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.400
entire British Isles, and, as such, he wanted
to strengthen the bond between all the places

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:25.150
and peoples he ruled over by creating a quote
‘union of hearts and minds’. One of the

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:30.270
ways James tried to foster this union of hearts
and minds was by promoting and financing the

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:35.650
education of protestant students at Oxford
that came from all over the British isles,

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:41.699
from Ireland to Scotland, and even Jersey.
By doing this, James hoped to foster unity

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:47.060
among all these people, of mostly aristocratic
blood, who would then either go back home

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.790
or partake in imperial expeditions abroad,
depending on where they went, helping the

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:55.870
crown either retain or expand imperial control.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.550
It was also at this point that the University
started to play another important role for

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:05.720
the empire. A more scholarly one. The gradual
development of the British Empire brought

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:10.710
with it also the development of a British
Imperial ideology; and it was the academic

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:16.450
environment at the University of Oxford which
proved particularly favorable for debating,

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:21.160
expanding, and eventually promulgating this
developing imperial ideology.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:26.180
For example, during James’ support of the
university in order to foster a ‘union of

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:31.080
hearts and minds’, Samuel Purchas, who was
educated at the other place but also became

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.281
a fellow at Oxford, wrote about how the British
Empire is an empire of not just English, but

00:07:36.281 --> 00:07:41.860
also of Protestant Irish, Scottish, and Welsh
peoples. Purchas basically used the academic

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:46.710
setting of the university to teach and publish
writings that supported the narrative and

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:52.131
ideology that James was trying to foster.
Purchas also supported the Empire, writing

00:07:52.131 --> 00:07:57.860
that all protestant British people ought to
go colonize the new world together for Britain.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:03.860
He was echoed in this by another Oxford professor,
and geographer, Richard Hakluyt, who also

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:09.389
wrote and taught in support of colonization
in North America. However, unlike Samuel Purchas,

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:15.300
Hakluyt emphasized more the idea of just ‘English
exceptionalism’ and the English Christenizing

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.520
and civilizing duty against native ‘savagery’.

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:23.229
This way the University of Oxford’s connection
to the empire became not only through its

00:08:23.229 --> 00:08:28.300
graduates but also through some of its academic
output. With that said, the University was

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:33.630
still an independent institution, and, even
though often heavily affected by the state’s

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:38.069
agenda and contemporary society, it did not
always have scholars that would present a

00:08:38.069 --> 00:08:43.490
coherent ‘empire is good’ argument, nor
did all the students and professors at the

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:48.589
university suddenly start to solely be interested
in imperial topics. Most Oxford fellows and

00:08:48.589 --> 00:08:53.060
graduates were still mostly concerned with
the clerical profession. It wouldn’t really

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:58.300
be until the 19th century that Oxford's connection
with the Empire became fully internalized

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.880
throughout the university.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.520
Nevertheless, this valuable role the University
of Oxford played, both educating the ruling

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.560
elite and providing an academic setting for
imperial thought to develop, was recognized

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:14.580
and supported by every subsequent British
ruler after James. Even during the brief time

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.420
of the Protectorate, Cromwell, just like the
monarchs before him, reaffirmed the university's

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:24.420
important role for the Empire and sought graduates
from the University (and Cambridge) for his

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:25.779
state’s service.

00:09:25.779 --> 00:09:30.620
It should, therefore, not be surprising that
when it came to the ever increasing colonial

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:36.370
British efforts in the Americas, Oxford men
were represented in high numbers. Walter Raleigh

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:42.231
founded Virginia, William Penn founded Pennsylvania,
and James Oglethorpe founded Georgia, all

00:09:42.231 --> 00:09:46.790
Oxford alumni. In fact 5 out of the first
9 Governors (initially just called presidents

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:51.980
of the company) of the Virginia Company Colony
were Oxford graduates. Another Oxford graduate,

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:56.750
John Lock, who was called ‘the Father of
Liberalism’, was also the secretary to the

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:02.110
Council of Trade and Plantations of the Carolinas,
and he helped draft the Constitution of the

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:07.830
Carolinas; which gave the Carolina planters
absolute power over their enslaved chattel

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:12.880
property. John Lock was also instrumental
in setting up the legal groundworks for ‘the

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.970
expropriation of aboriginal lands in America’
which was used by the British, and later the

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.620
American, government for centuries.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:25.060
Speaking of the Carolinas, it was a colony
in which another notable Oxford graduate,

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.170
and later also the University’s Chancellor,
had a large financial stake in, Edward Hyde

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:36.000
the first Earl of Clarendon. In fact, what
became South Carolina was known for a while

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.810
as Clarendon province. Clarendon was also
involved in the conquest of Ireland, and was

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:47.640
later in his life accused of using his political
influence to procure and sell charters for

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:53.500
the settlement of Ireland, making him a lot
of money. He also once stated that 'No man

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:58.970
… is more solicitous to establish Ireland
upon a true protestant English interest than

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:04.760
I am’. Clarendon was also a prolific writer
and at the end of his life he donated the

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:09.730
royalties from his books to the University
of Oxford, which used the profits to build

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:15.320
the Clarendon building, which you’re looking
at right now, with his statue right there.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:20.590
The amount of money the university made from
donations from people like Clarendon is hard

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:26.200
to determine. All the way up until 1870 the
University didn’t really keep consistent

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:31.590
accounting books and some donations, like
in the case of Clarendon, weren't always direct

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:37.060
cash, making it even more complicated to track.
Not to mention that some accounting documents

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:43.120
got simply lost or destroyed over time. However,
it is unquestionable that some of the donations

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:48.550
the university received throughout the centuries
came from imperialists, from slave owners,

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:50.540
slave traders, and others.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:55.339
With that said, the inconsistent accounting
books that do remain show that the University's

00:11:55.339 --> 00:12:00.510
wealth actually came largely from investments
and holdings it had accrued over the course

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:06.380
of its long existence, not from donations.
Large majority of these holdings and investments

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:11.810
where in England but the university also invested
in some of the colonial companies of the time

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:17.390
like the South Sea Company or the Royal African
Company, both of which primarily traded in

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:24.040
African slaves. This should perhaps not be
surprising as, for example, 4 of the 24 founding

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:28.370
officers of the Royal African Company were
Oxford alumni.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:33.589
The most well known Oxford alumnus imperialist
who made his fortune from slavery is Christopher

00:12:33.589 --> 00:12:39.860
Codrington, one of the largest 17th century
slave owners of the Brititsh West Indies.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:45.480
When Codrington died in 1710 he donated a
large sum of his money and books to the University

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:51.950
of Oxford All Souls college which promptly
used that money to build this library with

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:57.390
a Roman style statue of him in the middle.
The library has been known ever since as the

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:02.830
Codrington Library. Well, at least up until
2020, when the Condrington name was finally

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:07.800
dropped due to the fact that the money he
donated to the University was made from Slave

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:13.700
labor. The Roman style statue of him, however,
still remains inside the library.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:19.440
Another way the University gained money was
by patrons funding professorships or scholarships.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.980
Sometimes these were funded by the parliament,
sometimes by the monarchy, sometimes by rich

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:29.850
individuals, and sometimes by companies. For
example, the East India Company funded several

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:36.019
scholarships in the 17th century. Securing
these funds wasn’t very hard for the university

00:13:36.019 --> 00:13:39.339
considering its expansive alumni network.

00:13:39.339 --> 00:13:43.820
The last way the University financially benefited
from the British Empire that I will talk about

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:48.930
is by the acquisition of knowledge and artifacts.
Some artifacts and books the University acquired

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:53.620
were purchased legally by the university,
or imperial traders which then donated it

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:59.420
to the university. Others, however, were purchased
illegally, or stolen, or acquired through

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:04.620
imperial conquest or coercion. Sometimes the
university wasn’t even involved and it was

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:09.300
the professors of the university that made
these deals on their own. And, sometimes,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:15.370
like in the case of the 17th century Selden
Map of China or the 16th century Codex Bodley,

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:20.639
we don’t really know how they got acquired.
However, as the British Empire spread across

00:14:20.639 --> 00:14:26.240
the world, it is undeniable that the University
benefited from the knowledge and artifacts

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.459
brought back to Britain. Some of the University
professors even had agreements with colonial

00:14:31.459 --> 00:14:36.870
companies, like the Levant Company or the
East Indian Company, to bring them back all

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:40.839
the books they acquired on their expeditions.

00:14:40.839 --> 00:14:46.839
In the 18th century, Oxford professors and
alumni continued to play vital roles all around

00:14:46.839 --> 00:14:52.399
the British Empire. For example, William Jones
and Robert Chambers were Oxford professors

00:14:52.399 --> 00:14:57.880
who were sent, in the latter half of the 18th
century, to Bengal, to act as local judges

00:14:57.880 --> 00:15:02.959
for the East India Company. While there, William
Jones became the first British scholar of

00:15:02.959 --> 00:15:08.720
Sanskrit and Robert Chambers was one of the
judges that presided over the notorious Maharaja

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.350
Nandakumar case.

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:15.480
To explain, Maharaja Nandakumar was said to
have been the first Indian sentenced to be

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:22.130
hanged by the British after an official British
court case in 1775. Meaning it signaled the

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:27.720
ever growing control the British government
had over parts of India. The court case itself

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:34.290
was a sham. Nandakumar, an Indian tax collector
hired by the East India Company, accused the

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:40.240
first British Governor General of Bengal,
Warren Hastings, of corruption. He even provided

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:45.930
several documents to prove this. Hasting quickly
denied this and accused Nandakumar of forgery

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:51.550
of the said documents. Rather than Hastings
being tried, it was Nandakumar who was sent

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:56.339
to court for forgery. Knowing the British
court would rule in favor of the Governor,

00:15:56.339 --> 00:16:01.790
Nandakumar asked to be judged by a jury of
his peers as allowed under the English common

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:07.850
law. The court obliged but then went and filled
the jury with only British people who didn’t

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:13.519
even know the local language and had to have
an interpreter during the entire trial. Naturally

00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:19.959
this phony trial ruled in favor of Hastings
and sentenced Nandakumar to death. This trial

00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:25.649
set the precedent for what the Indians could
expect if they dared raise legal issues against

00:16:25.649 --> 00:16:32.540
British officials. A contemporary British
MP, Edmund Burke, stated ‘The accuser, they

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:39.050
saw hanged. [While] the culprit, without acquittal
or enquiry, triumphs on the ground of that

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:45.339
murder: a murder not of Nandakumar only. But
of all living testimony, and even of evidence

00:16:45.339 --> 00:16:50.050
yet unborn. From that time not a complaint
has been heard from the natives against their

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:51.360
governor.’

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:56.350
It wasn’t just in India, however, where
Oxford judges took part in prominent imperial

00:16:56.350 --> 00:17:02.070
court cases. Back home, in England, the practice
of Slavery by the British Empire had started

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:07.270
to be hotly debated by the end of the 18th
century. The University of Oxford’s relationship

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:12.459
with slavery was a complicated one. On the
one hand, the University definitely benefited

00:17:12.459 --> 00:17:17.620
from the money being generated by slave labor,
whether through investments, donations, or

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:23.449
payments from students that came from slave
owning families. But, on the other hand, the

00:17:23.449 --> 00:17:27.999
university also had prominent abolitionist
students, professors, and even awarded an

00:17:27.999 --> 00:17:33.429
honorary degree to James Beattie for his work
against slavery. Probably the most notable

00:17:33.429 --> 00:17:40.279
Oxford abolitionist, well, sort of, was William
Murray, the 1st Earl of Mansfield. He was

00:17:40.279 --> 00:17:46.530
the judge in the 1772 Somersett’s Case which,
for the first time in English history, stated

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:52.440
that there was no precedent for slavery in
English common law. This did not outlaw slavery

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:58.279
in the Empire as a whole but it did make slaves
residing in England technically free of their

00:17:58.279 --> 00:18:03.480
master’s control. Mansfield, in the Somersett’s
Case, upheld the interpretation of English

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:08.570
common law championed by another contemporary
Oxford legal Professor, William Blackstone,

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:14.019
who stated that slavery was ‘repugnant to
natural law and reason’ and that ‘pure

00:18:14.019 --> 00:18:18.989
and proper slavery does not, nay cannot, subsist
in England’.

00:18:18.989 --> 00:18:24.110
With that said, abolishing slavery in the
whole Empire was a slow porcess and it took

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:28.850
well over another half a century for it to
truly be abolished. This was partly because

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:34.200
slave owners like Charles Ellis, an Oxford
graduate, had large amounts of wealth which

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:40.080
they used extensively for lobbying in defense
of slavery. One of the lobbying tactics used

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:46.879
by the planters centered around the ever increasing
18th century recialization of the world. In

00:18:46.879 --> 00:18:52.059
essence, many British planters started to
argue their right for owning slaves came from

00:18:52.059 --> 00:18:58.899
their racial superiority. This idea of racial
superiority came from an 18th century development

00:18:58.899 --> 00:19:05.950
of a British Imperial ideology of Anglo-Saxon,
or Teutonic, or just British exceptionalism.

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:12.090
The ideology of British or English exceptionalism
wasn’t, however, only based on race. It

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:18.669
also included a belief in superior English
culture, religion, civic development, intellect,

00:19:18.669 --> 00:19:24.440
science, etc. This idea of English exceptionalism
can be traced to the earliest days of the

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:29.789
British Empire, but it was in the 18th century
that it truly solidified in the British society.

00:19:29.789 --> 00:19:34.899
Oxford's scholars weren’t as active in the
creation of this Imperial ideology as for

00:19:34.899 --> 00:19:40.000
example Scottish Universities’ graduates
were, but, some, like William Pitt the elder

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:45.409
and Edward Gibbon, both of whom went to Oxford
but did not finish their studies, did play

00:19:45.409 --> 00:19:46.510
their part.

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:51.769
William Pitt was championed by his contemporaries
to be ‘a Minister, to whose efforts the

00:19:51.769 --> 00:19:58.259
British Empire owes its safety and its fame’,
for he ‘armed the hardy race of the north’

00:19:58.259 --> 00:20:03.519
because ‘the north has ever been propitious
to conquest’. Gibbon reaffirmed such statements

00:20:03.519 --> 00:20:09.210
of a hardy race of the north in his historical
writings where he once wrote that ‘we [the

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:15.109
English] may assume a conspicuous place among
all the inhabitants of the earth’.

00:20:15.109 --> 00:20:19.999
This ‘conspicuous’ place of the English
in the world, this propitiousness of theirs

00:20:19.999 --> 00:20:26.450
to conquest, this exceptionalism of theirs,
was then used in the 19th and early 20th century

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:32.200
to justify the British Empire. In other words,
because the Anglo-Saxons, the English, were

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:37.270
perceived by themselves as so exceptional
and superior, and because Britain was seen

00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:42.480
as being so ‘Great’, it ought to colonize
as much of the world as possible for the quote

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:47.750
‘common good’. It was the English burden,
which later became known as the ‘white man’s

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:53.359
burden’, to spread their perceived superior
culture, religion, language, and race around

00:20:53.359 --> 00:21:00.110
the world to, theoretically, eventually raise
all the other peoples of the world to their

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:01.110
level.

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:06.090
It was this 19th century Imperial ideology
of a ‘white man’s burden’, which developed

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:10.789
from the ideology of English exceptionalism,
that the University of Oxford played a central

00:21:10.789 --> 00:21:17.820
role in devising and promulgating. John Ruskin,
an Oxford arts professor, lectured on how

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:23.149
England ‘must found colonies as fast and
as far as she is able, formed of her most

00:21:23.149 --> 00:21:28.739
energetic and worthiest men;—seizing every
piece of fruitful waste ground she can set

00:21:28.739 --> 00:21:35.240
her foot on’. James Froude, an Oxford history
professor, stated that ‘when the inequality

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:40.460
[between the colonizers and the natives] has
been proved beyond dispute by long and painful

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:46.519
experience - the wisdom, and ultimately the
duty, of the weaker party is to accept the

00:21:46.519 --> 00:21:51.909
benefits which are offered in exchange for
submission.’ Froude also echoed the speech

00:21:51.909 --> 00:21:58.169
of Thomas Macaulay who stated, ‘It may be
that … by good government we may educate

00:21:58.169 --> 00:22:04.289
our subjects into a capacity for better government,
that, having become instructed in European

00:22:04.289 --> 00:22:09.740
knowledge they may in some future age demand
European institutions … The scepter may

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:15.669
pass away from us but … there is an Empire
exempt’. Basically, the civilizing mission,

00:22:15.669 --> 00:22:20.159
which is the ‘white man’s burden’, was
seen as exempting the British Empire from

00:22:20.159 --> 00:22:25.460
the conquest, violence, and exploitation of
the natives. In this context, the British

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:31.590
patted themselves on the back when they finally
abolished slavery in 1834, which was seen

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:38.210
as part of their civilizing mission of uplifting
the poorer races from slavery. The idea of

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:43.759
racial superiority still remained, but it
was no longer used to justify slavery, it

00:22:43.759 --> 00:22:49.230
was now used to justify the world’s racial
hierachy with the English and their civlizing

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:51.750
Empire being at the top.

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:58.340
Robert Southey, William Stubbs, Alfred Milner,
Charles Lucas, Albert Dicey, Edward Freeman,

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:04.169
Reginald Coupland and many many more were
all Oxford men championing the idea of the

00:23:04.169 --> 00:23:09.299
white man’s burden. As historian Brockliss
states ‘Most influential dons (Oxford professors)

00:23:09.299 --> 00:23:12.039
were ardent Imperialists.’

00:23:12.039 --> 00:23:17.349
Now Britain was not unique in justifying its
Empire with the argument of a ‘civilizing

00:23:17.349 --> 00:23:21.869
mission’. Almost every 19th century Empire
did so. But such whataboutist arguments don't

00:23:21.869 --> 00:23:26.499
really excuse the actions. Up until now I
have talked about the British Empire as it

00:23:26.499 --> 00:23:31.109
pertains to the University of Oxford, and
I shall continue doing that. But, I will recommend

00:23:31.109 --> 00:23:35.999
that you read this book Legacy of Violence
:&nbsp;A

00:23:35.999 --> 00:23:42.159
History of the British Empire This book is
great if you want to learn just how much violence

00:23:42.159 --> 00:23:47.869
was integral to the British Empire. And, it
was written by a Harvard Professor.

00:23:47.869 --> 00:23:52.460
With that said, there were also some Oxford
men who tried to fight against the persistent

00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:58.460
racism and imperial violence of the time.
William Tozer, an Oxford graduate, was appointed

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:03.080
as the head of the Universities’ Christian
Mission to Africa. He focused on converting

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:08.370
the locals and curtailing the Islamic Slave
trade. Unlike most British people in Africa,

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:14.210
Tozer never used violence and he always tried
to work with the local African institutions

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:19.850
rather than against them. Also, while in Africa,
Tozer noticed that many of the English where

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:25.210
blatantly racist towards the Africans, he
stated that the ‘Englishmen generally have

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:30.059
a very much less kindly feeling towards a
free negro (black) than the Arabs have towards

00:24:30.059 --> 00:24:36.239
their slaves.’ Many Englishmen talk of freeing
the Africans from Arab slavery only to then

00:24:36.239 --> 00:24:42.399
treat them worse than the Arab slavers. This
statement was, of course, a hyperbole to prove

00:24:42.399 --> 00:24:45.710
a point but it was based in truth.

00:24:45.710 --> 00:24:50.080
Coming back to the University of Oxford. The
role of the university as a place of education

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:56.190
for the empire’s elite and as a place for
the creation and promotion of imperial ideology,

00:24:56.190 --> 00:25:01.639
truly reached its zenith in the 19th and early
20th century. As Brockliss states when talking

00:25:01.639 --> 00:25:06.840
about the 19th century ‘It was considered
to be Oxford’s primary function to take

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:12.279
callow youths and turn them into intelligent,
upright, and dedicated servants of a British

00:25:12.279 --> 00:25:18.720
civilizing mission’. Basically, Oxford men
were expected and taught to take up the ‘white

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:22.620
man’s burden’.
Even Rudyard Kipling, the person who popularized

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:27.179
the term the ‘white man’s burden’ with
his infamous ‘white man’s burden poem’

00:25:27.179 --> 00:25:33.549
was nominated for the nobel prize in literature
by an Oxford professor, Charles Oman. Oman

00:25:33.549 --> 00:25:39.440
himself wrote a book on English history where
in the last sentence of the 740 page book

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:46.519
he concluded with, ‘the future of the world
lies in the hands of the Anglo-Saxon race’.

00:25:46.519 --> 00:25:51.289
The influence the university of Oxford graduates
had over the British Empire in the 19th and

00:25:51.289 --> 00:25:56.489
early 20th century was truly astounding. As
historian Caroline Elkins put it, ‘Given

00:25:56.489 --> 00:26:01.639
the sheer size of Britain’s far-flung empire,
it was impossible to micromanage day-to-day

00:26:01.639 --> 00:26:07.769
rule in these areas. Instead, the prevailing
ethos was to “trust the man on the spot,”

00:26:07.769 --> 00:26:13.789
which officials in London did with great regularity.
In practice, this meant a very small cadre

00:26:13.789 --> 00:26:19.450
of recruits joined the empire’s civil service,
coming from mostly schools such as Oxford

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:20.969
and Cambridge’.

00:26:20.969 --> 00:26:25.899
Just how many Imperial recruits actually came
from Oxford? Well, ‘Over the whole period

00:26:25.899 --> 00:26:32.289
of British rule in India, 15 Governor-Generals
or Viceroys came from Oxford’. Also, between

00:26:32.289 --> 00:26:40.700
1895 and 1914 around 50% of all successful
applicants for either home or colonial civil

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:46.789
service came from Oxford. This also created
a positive feedback loop through the old boys

00:26:46.789 --> 00:26:51.469
network. That is, the more Oxford men were
employed in the British Imperial Service the

00:26:51.469 --> 00:26:57.450
more Oxford men were hired. As Richard Symonds
points out, ‘the examinations for entrance

00:26:57.450 --> 00:27:03.779
to the top grade of the home and Indian civil
service were designed to advantage Oxford

00:27:03.779 --> 00:27:09.039
graduates’ mainly because they were designed
by Oxford graduates. Toynbee Junior, an Oxford

00:27:09.039 --> 00:27:13.960
fellow, even stated that if a student came
to Oxford not knowing what he wanted to do

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:19.259
in life, he would simply be signed up for
the Indian Civil Service, because quote ‘An

00:27:19.259 --> 00:27:23.340
Indian civil service career was assured to
any … [Oxford] man who could not do better

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:24.659
than that.’

00:27:24.659 --> 00:27:29.169
The benefits the University of Oxford saw
from all its graduates becoming integral parts

00:27:29.169 --> 00:27:34.850
of the Imperial state were tremendous. Not
only did most of its students have a guaranteed

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:40.960
job, if they wished to take it, but Oxford
became the imperial school, sometimes even

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:46.960
at the cost of colonial schools. For example,
George Curzon, an Oxford graduate and the

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:51.739
viceroy of India, who also later became the
University of Oxford’s chancellor, closed

00:27:51.739 --> 00:27:57.700
down India’s School of Forestry at Cooper’s
Hill and relocated it, along with its staff,

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:04.019
to the University of Oxford. Oxford's forestry
school, right behind me, was still funded

00:28:04.019 --> 00:28:10.960
by the Indian Colonial government but it was
now in Oxford, for the benefit of Oxford students.

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:15.679
Other subjects taught in Oxford, and other
British universities of the time, which could

00:28:15.679 --> 00:28:21.419
not be studied in India included Anthropology,
Mineralogy, and Geology. The University of

00:28:21.419 --> 00:28:27.009
Oxford also built The Indian Institute which
was to help educate the future public servants

00:28:27.009 --> 00:28:28.729
of India (all in Oxford).

00:28:28.729 --> 00:28:33.279
Of course, if Indians wished to study all
these subjects they theoretically could have

00:28:33.279 --> 00:28:38.989
applied to Oxford but their chances of getting
in were much smaller than for any British

00:28:38.989 --> 00:28:44.369
student. One University fellow, Herbert Blakiston
(Trinity), even stated in 1913, ‘I should

00:28:44.369 --> 00:28:49.639
say that we … are not anxious to encourage
Indian students to come to Oxford’. The

00:28:49.639 --> 00:28:53.909
fact that Curzon later became the chancellor
of the University also didn’t help the Indians

00:28:53.909 --> 00:28:59.570
studying at Oxford. Curzon held quite racist
beliefs against the Indians ever since his

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:06.450
viceroyalty when he stated that all Indians
are ‘less than schoolchildren’, and while

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:10.799
being the chancellor of the university he
was said to have been constantly paranoid

00:29:10.799 --> 00:29:15.929
about the loyalty of the Indian students.
Some Indian students were even afraid of submitting

00:29:15.929 --> 00:29:21.300
papers in modern history classes because they
thought the essays would be sent to the colonial

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:24.909
office in order to judge their loyalties to
the Empire.

00:29:24.909 --> 00:29:30.160
This kind of prejudice and racism wasn’t,
of course, only against Indian students. All

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:36.359
potential Oxford students deemed as ‘non-white’
had to fight against quite racist sentiment

00:29:36.359 --> 00:29:42.289
if they wished to study at Oxford. And, keep
in mind, by design, Oxford, along with other

00:29:42.289 --> 00:29:47.210
English universities of the time, was the
only place in the empire where you could study

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:53.719
certain subjects. Therefore, of course, the
most qualified people for higher office jobs

00:29:53.719 --> 00:29:57.899
ended up being, for the most part, British.

00:29:57.899 --> 00:30:02.450
Another way the University benefited from
the Empire was through the university press.

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:07.909
As the Empire expanded so did the reach of
the Oxford University press. From here, at

00:30:07.909 --> 00:30:13.320
Walton Street, the Oxford University Press
managed branches all across the empire. Easily

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:18.179
securing distribution and print licenses thanks
to the university’s alumni network. This

00:30:18.179 --> 00:30:22.840
way the University Printing Press indirectly
hindered the development of local knowledge

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:30.149
distribution in favor of Oxford's knowledge
distribution. This knowledge was Anglocentric

00:30:30.149 --> 00:30:35.279
and sometimes quite racist. For example, in
1911, the University press started printing

00:30:35.279 --> 00:30:41.539
this book for primary schools. It’s called
A History of England written by an Oxford

00:30:41.539 --> 00:30:48.320
history professor C. R. L. Fletcher. In it,
Fletcher describes Ireland as a ‘spoilt

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:53.179
child’ who is ‘poor, disloyal, and ignorant’,
he goes on to say that the descendence of

00:30:53.179 --> 00:30:58.919
African slaves in the carribean are ‘lazy,
vicious and incapable of any serious improvement

00:30:58.919 --> 00:31:06.210
… and quite useless’, black South Africans
are described as ‘fierce savages’, and

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:12.110
the aboriginals of Australia are ‘nothing
but a few miserable blacks’. Fletcher then

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:19.580
concludes with ‘the natives everywhere welcome
the mercy and justice of our rule.’ … This

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:27.320
book was being actively printed by the University’s
press until 1954. Guyana, after it gained

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:33.950
independence from the British, even banned
the book because it was deemed too racist.

00:31:33.950 --> 00:31:39.899
When it came to the British colonies in Southern
Africa, here too, Oxford fellows were represented

00:31:39.899 --> 00:31:45.850
in large numbers. For example, an Oxford graduate,
Alfred Milner, became the governor of the

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:51.460
Cape Colony, and later the High Commissioner
for Southern Africa. During his time in Africa,

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:57.379
Milner’s entire staff was almost exclusively
made up of Oxford graduates, which outsiders

00:31:57.379 --> 00:32:03.390
called Milner’s Oxford ‘kindergarten’.
Milner worked closely with Cecil Rhodes, another

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:09.999
Oxford graduate, and several other Imperialists,
to develop the foundation for a quote ‘wholesale

00:32:09.999 --> 00:32:15.429
exploitation of black labor in the white owned
mines, and the rise of the apartheid regime

00:32:15.429 --> 00:32:21.609
that, decades later, would come to power on
the racist coattails of Milner’s Oxford

00:32:21.609 --> 00:32:27.010
ceoterie’. Milner also send a letter to
his Oxford friend Asquith upon his arrival

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:32.289
to South Africa in which he stated that ‘You
have only to sacrifice the nigger (blacks)

00:32:32.289 --> 00:32:34.899
absolutely … and then the game is easy’.

00:32:34.899 --> 00:32:40.959
However, it was Milner’s friend, Cecil Rhodes,
who is seen as the most notorious South African

00:32:40.959 --> 00:32:47.700
Imperialist. You have probably seen this picture
before, specially in the context of African

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:54.759
Colonialism, well that picture is the Rhodes
Colossus, that is Cecil Rhodes. Rhodes was

00:32:54.759 --> 00:32:59.919
the quintessential British Imperialist, he
believed in the white man’s burden narrative,

00:32:59.919 --> 00:33:05.090
he believed in the superiority of the Anglo-Saxon
race, he organized and conducted brutal for

00:33:05.090 --> 00:33:11.249
profit conquests of today's Zambia and Zimbabwe,
and, as the Prime Minister of the Cape colony,

00:33:11.249 --> 00:33:17.509
enacted segregationist laws which helped,
along with Milner’s laws, the beginnings

00:33:17.509 --> 00:33:23.709
of the racist apartheid regime in South Africa.
Rhodes even stated that ‘you must govern

00:33:23.709 --> 00:33:26.359
the natives … as a subject race’.

00:33:26.359 --> 00:33:31.220
During an 1896 rebellion in today’s Zimbabwe
Rhodes, against the orders of the British

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:36.779
government, personally led an army to crush
the rebellion. He implemented brutal scorched

00:33:36.779 --> 00:33:42.590
earth tactics ordering his officers to quote
‘do the most harm you can to the natives

00:33:42.590 --> 00:33:45.779
around you … kill all you can’.

00:33:45.779 --> 00:33:50.950
Rhodes financed his political and imperial
campaigns with the money of his business partners

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:56.690
like Alfred Beit, and with his own money which
he primarily made from South African and later

00:33:56.690 --> 00:34:01.981
Rhodesian Mines. These mines were run on the
‘compound system’ which often (but not

00:34:01.981 --> 00:34:07.269
always) resembled slavery in all but name.
To quote Rob Turrell, ‘Compounds provided

00:34:07.269 --> 00:34:13.270
the framework for the total control of African
workers. Once inside these military-style

00:34:13.270 --> 00:34:19.349
barracks, workers lost all access to the outside
world for the length of a contract.’ While

00:34:19.349 --> 00:34:25.179
in these compounds many workers were also
under the supervision of white ‘overseers’

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:30.440
called ‘masters’. An African technically
did not have to take up this contractual job

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:35.849
but Rhodes, as the Prime Minister of the Cape
Colony, implemented the Hut Tux, and more

00:34:35.849 --> 00:34:42.069
importantly, the Glen Grey Act, which stated
that ‘Every male black person … who in

00:34:42.069 --> 00:34:48.549
the magistrate's opinion was capable of labour,
had to pay a tax of ten shillings per year,

00:34:48.549 --> 00:34:53.790
but would not have to pay the tax if he had
worked.’ This meant that Africans had to

00:34:53.790 --> 00:35:00.269
take up jobs in order to not pay extra taxes
or even to have money to pay taxes, and almost

00:35:00.269 --> 00:35:04.789
all jobs for blacks in South Africa were contractual
jobs.

00:35:04.789 --> 00:35:09.549
When Rhodes died he gave a large sum of the
money he made from his mines and Imperial

00:35:09.549 --> 00:35:14.760
activities to the University of Oxford. Part
of the money went to establish the Rhodes

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:20.660
Scholarship with the Rhodes House behind me
and part of it was given to Oriel College

00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:24.930
Oxford which built this building with a statue
of Rhodes at the top.

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:30.119
Rhodes, however, wasn’t the only South African
Imperialists to donate money to the University.

00:35:30.119 --> 00:35:36.220
Abe Bailey, for example, funded a library
and a reading room for Oxford students studying

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:42.049
Colonial Geography and History. Bailey, just
like Rhodes, made most of his money in mines

00:35:42.049 --> 00:35:47.390
run on the aforementioned compound system.
In particular, Bailey made a lot of money

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:53.010
from Chinese indechoured laborers who worked
in such terrible conditions that it became

00:35:53.010 --> 00:35:57.330
known in South Africa as quote ‘chinese
slavery’.

00:35:57.330 --> 00:36:02.990
Another Southern African Imperialist and Oxford
benefactor was Alfred Beit, aforementioned

00:36:02.990 --> 00:36:08.170
business partner of Rhodes. Beit endowed the
first professorship and lectureship in colonial

00:36:08.170 --> 00:36:13.920
history at Oxford. The first historian to
take up this post was Hugh Egerton who once

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:20.130
stated that ‘Every school building [at Oxford]
is a citadel of Empire and every teacher its

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:25.440
sentinel.’ He also said that ‘It is equally
false to suggest that our empire took its

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:31.329
rise in violence. What happened was peaceful
occupation of, apparently, vacant lands’.

00:36:31.329 --> 00:36:36.910
All of this, however, does not mean that everyone
at the University of Oxford was an imperialist

00:36:36.910 --> 00:36:43.200
or a racist. Cornelia Sorabji, the first Indian
woman to come to study at Oxford, and also

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:47.599
the first woman at Oxford to study law, talked
about how even though she had to fight some

00:36:47.599 --> 00:36:52.040
prejudice and racism at Oxford, there were
also people at Oxford that helped her fight

00:36:52.040 --> 00:36:56.850
against that prejudice and racism. For example,
when she obtained the highest marks of her

00:36:56.850 --> 00:37:01.940
class from Mumbai University, which ought
to qualify her for a scholarship at Oxford,

00:37:01.940 --> 00:37:07.099
she was denied this spot because not only
was she an Indian, but she was also a woman.

00:37:07.099 --> 00:37:12.470
However, hearing of this, Lord Hobhouse, an
Oxford graduate, helped her secure a different

00:37:12.470 --> 00:37:17.150
scholarship at Oxford (at Somerville College).
Then, while at Oxford, she was initially denied

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:22.980
the opportunity to take the legal exams because
the examiners refused to test a woman, but,

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:27.500
when she told this to Benjamin Jowett, the
vice chancellor of the university, he made

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:32.589
sure that Sorabji could take the exam just
like any other student. Sorabji would later

00:37:32.589 --> 00:37:36.660
write that she would never forget the ‘kindness
of everyone’ at Oxford who helped her and

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:39.589
that she ‘loved’ her time at the University.

00:37:39.589 --> 00:37:44.589
This dichotomy of experiences, Sorabji and
many other colonial ‘non-white’ students

00:37:44.589 --> 00:37:50.180
like Grantley Adams or Norman Manley faced,
was a common theme at the University. On the

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:54.809
one hand, there were students and faculty
members that treated them as friends and tried

00:37:54.809 --> 00:38:00.859
helping them and, on the other, the University’s
system often worked against them and some

00:38:00.859 --> 00:38:06.460
students and faculty members were outright
antagonistic and racist to them.

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:10.809
This was especially true if you tried to go
against the established white man’s burden

00:38:10.809 --> 00:38:15.500
narrative, like in the case of Eric Williams,
who later became the first prime minister

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:21.200
of Trinidad and Tobago. Williams studied history
at Oxford and for his doctorate thesis in

00:38:21.200 --> 00:38:27.160
1938 he wrote about The Economic Aspects of
the Abolition of the Slave Trade and West

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:32.819
Indian Slavery. In the writing of his thesis
he was helped by his advisor and by some of

00:38:32.819 --> 00:38:38.049
the university’s fellows, but over all,
his questioning of the idea that slavery was

00:38:38.049 --> 00:38:43.680
abolished due to economic reasons and not
moral ones was extremely controversial because

00:38:43.680 --> 00:38:49.549
it went against the white man’s burden narrative.
Today the scale of the economic argument for

00:38:49.549 --> 00:38:54.549
the abolishment of slavery is still being
debated, but no current historians are arguing

00:38:54.549 --> 00:38:59.670
there was no economic influence, which is
something that most Williams’ contemporaries

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:06.130
didn’t even want to entertain let alone
debate. Williams’ thesis was so controversial,

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:11.769
in fact, that every British publisher, including
the press of the university where he wrote

00:39:11.769 --> 00:39:17.789
the doctoral thesis at, refused to publish
his work. This was still during the time when

00:39:17.789 --> 00:39:23.200
the University press was publishing the aforementioned
History of England book.

00:39:23.200 --> 00:39:25.109
(long cut)

00:39:25.109 --> 00:39:30.589
As the British Empire ‘formerly’ fell
apart in the latter half of the 20th century,

00:39:30.589 --> 00:39:36.031
Oxford’s active connection to the Empire
also fell apart and the University, under

00:39:36.031 --> 00:39:42.780
the rather accurate accusations of only producing
‘an anti-technological, arts-trained Oxbridge

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:48.680
dilettante elite’, had to undergo restructuring.
As the university could no longer count on

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:54.420
drawing its student body from the Empire’s
elite and sending it back to govern the Empire,

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:59.570
it began to shift its priorities. First, the
university became more global and started

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:04.490
to draw students, and also faculty members,
from all over the world, not just from the

00:40:04.490 --> 00:40:09.869
anglophone sphere. And, second, the university
started to focus more on being a research

00:40:09.869 --> 00:40:15.869
institution with frequent academic publications,
all while trying to become more inclusive

00:40:15.869 --> 00:40:19.819
through various Oxford outreach initiatives.

00:40:19.819 --> 00:40:24.630
The British Empire of the 19th and 20th century
is gone, and the University's connection to

00:40:24.630 --> 00:40:31.990
it is part of history now. However, certain
Imperial legacies do remain, such as the statue

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:37.181
of Codrington in the All Souls Library, or
the statue of Rhodes at Oriel College, or

00:40:37.181 --> 00:40:43.190
the name of the Rhodes’ Scholarship, or
the name of the Beit’s Professorship, etc.

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:49.930
These names and statues act as symbols of
the University’s Imperial past, a past that

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:55.670
means different things to different people.
To some, it symbolizes the greatness of the

00:40:55.670 --> 00:41:01.880
British Empire while to others it symbolizes
the violence and racism the Empire inflicted,

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:07.240
the legacy of which can still be felt to this
day. It is the latter reason why movements

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:13.141
like ‘Rhodes Must Fall’ have organized
protests against Rhodes’ legacy at the University

00:41:13.141 --> 00:41:14.630
of Oxford.

00:41:14.630 --> 00:41:20.380
Why some people might feel particularly angry
about Rhodes can perhaps be best summarized

00:41:20.380 --> 00:41:27.750
by Trevor Noah who, in his book Born a Crime,
stated ‘There is also this to consider:

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:33.680
The name Hitler does not offend a black South
African because Hitler is not the worst thing

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:40.549
a black South African can imagine. Every country
thinks their history is the most important,

00:41:40.549 --> 00:41:46.339
and that's especially true in the West. But
if black South Africans could go back in time

00:41:46.339 --> 00:41:55.480
and kill one person, Cecil Rhodes would come
up before Hitler.’ Rhodes is not Hitler,

00:41:55.480 --> 00:42:02.520
but, just like Hitler, Rhodes does have a
legacy of violence, racism, and warmongering,

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:04.990
especially in Southern Africa.

00:42:04.990 --> 00:42:10.700
Which begs the question, why do some people
at Oxford, and in Britain at large, want to

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:12.819
defend his statue?

00:42:12.819 --> 00:42:20.099
Well, as Caroline Elkins states, ‘Questions
over whether “Rhodes Must Fall” are, of

00:42:20.099 --> 00:42:27.769
course, proxies for much larger battles over
how we in the present remember the past.’

00:42:27.769 --> 00:42:34.059
That is, the imperial legacy that is remembered
by some English people is not one of violence,

00:42:34.059 --> 00:42:40.029
racism, and slavery but one of building projects,
scientific breakthroughs, and christianizing

00:42:40.029 --> 00:42:41.400
missions.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:45.941
When some English people look at Rhodes’
Imperial legacy at Oxford they don’t see

00:42:45.941 --> 00:42:51.470
his violent actions in Africa as much as they
see the Grade 2 listed building he helped

00:42:51.470 --> 00:42:57.190
fund, they don’t see his slave like compound
system, as much as they see his scholarship

00:42:57.190 --> 00:43:03.579
helping fund students like Howard Florey;
who won the nobel prize for his work on penicillin,

00:43:03.579 --> 00:43:08.200
arguably one of the most important medical
discoveries of the 20th century. The changing

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:14.369
or taking down of symbols that, to some English
people, represent this positive memory is

00:43:14.369 --> 00:43:19.990
seen as erasing the ‘positive’ historical
aspects of the British Empire.

00:43:19.990 --> 00:43:24.990
However, no one is talking about destroying
the Grade 2 listed building, no one is talking

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:29.319
about defunding the Rhode’s Scholarship,
no one is saying that nothing good came from

00:43:29.319 --> 00:43:34.420
the British Empire, and no one is saying that
parts of the University, Condrington, and

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:40.339
other Imperialists aren’t products of their
time. Even the University press, which I mostly

00:43:40.339 --> 00:43:45.559
talked about in a negative light in this video,
has many positive aspects and published this

00:43:45.559 --> 00:43:51.289
book, Oxford and Empire, without which this
video would be impossible to make.

00:43:51.289 --> 00:43:56.619
What people who protest against imperial symbols
actually want is the recognition that some

00:43:56.619 --> 00:44:02.390
of these imperial symbols are products of
an unsavory past, and, therefore, should be

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:09.330
changed or taken down for they are quite anachronistic
in a modern society that views exploitation,

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:14.550
racism, slavery, and violence as things that
should not be praised.

00:44:14.550 --> 00:44:21.710
As historian Richard J Evans said ‘statues,
monuments and memorials … are there to remind

00:44:21.710 --> 00:44:29.269
us of who we are, or better put, who we want
to be as a nation. It is an obvious point

00:44:29.269 --> 00:44:38.079
that some memorials are no longer fit for
purpose.’ Yes, statues, monuments, and memorials

00:44:38.079 --> 00:44:44.369
symbolize the values of the society that put
them up but they also symbolize the values

00:44:44.369 --> 00:44:50.799
of the society that chooses to keep them up;
and if your values make you defend a statue

00:44:50.799 --> 00:44:56.720
of a man who once stated ‘kill all you can’
or ‘you must govern the natives … as a

00:44:56.720 --> 00:45:07.960
subject race’, then perhaps it is time to
rethink your values.

00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:08.960
...

00:45:08.960 --> 00:45:18.940
Agh, this is the second time I am here. Second
time I woke up at 4:30 am. Because last time

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:21.809
I forgot to plug in the microphone.

00:45:21.809 --> 00:45:27.589
It's good because there's no one here. Usually
during the day there's like a billion people

00:45:27.589 --> 00:45:30.529
around.

00:45:30.529 --> 00:45:34.380
The problematic nature is that I have to wake
up early, and even the more problematic nature

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:36.339
is that I have to do it twice.

00:45:36.339 --> 00:45:38.442
But, haha, hopefully the video is good.

00:45:38.442 --> 00:45:41.400
I hope you all enjoyed this video, I understand

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:46.359
this can be a quite contentious topic especially
in Britain, but I will conclude with this

00:45:46.359 --> 00:45:53.569
quote from Sorabji which I found quite nice
when researching; she wrote ‘Best of all,

00:45:53.569 --> 00:46:01.220
[at Oxford] I learnt that difference of opinion
need not affect friendship or personal appreciation.’

00:46:01.220 --> 00:46:05.230
So next time when you’re talking to people
about this topic or are even typing something

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:11.309
in the comment section right now, please remember,
there is no reason why we all can’t be civil

00:46:11.309 --> 00:46:17.440
when discussing the uncomfortable legacy of
the British empire.

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:27.880
or ... the uncomfortable parts of the British
Empire.

